      "problem": "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14123785/assets/4822a7252af596ce08cc4a38bcd8f9a1e5c9b99470b31e81778229b92c2e1da8.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
